            <ArticleTitle>[Imaging diagnosis of an unilateral painful ophtalmoplegia].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>792-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The ophtalmoplegia may be due to several lesions and the diagnosis is made by imaging modalities. We report the case of a giant aneurysm of the wright intracavernous internal carotid artery revealed by painful ophtalmoplegia with subarachnoid haemorrhage. The diagnosis was made by magnetic resonance imaging and angiography.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
